 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Progressive Encoding of Time-Varying Sequences](959-progressive-encoding-of-time-varying-sequences.md)
-A scheme for encoding time-varying volumetric data where frames are encoded individually but reuse codebooks from previous frames to improve performance and compression.
+Compresses time-varying volumetric data by encoding frames individually while reusing codebooks from previous frames. It distinguishes itself by introducing temporal dependencies (I-frames and P-frames), reducing memory and encoding time compared to independent frame encoding.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding (codebook generation) is iterative and can be slow, especially with the LBG algorithm [S3]. Decoding is $O(1)$ per vector (index lookup), making it suitable for real-time rendering [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage is $O(K \cdot n + N)$, where $K$ is codebook size, $n$ is vector dimension, and $N$ is the number of input vectors (stored as indices) [S2]. This is significantly smaller than storing $N \cdot n$ raw vectors [S2].
-*   **Performance:** High compression ratios are achieved for data with high spatial or temporal redundancy (e.g., highly sparse data or time-varying sequences) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast decoding; effective for high-dimensional data; portable via texture-based GPU implementation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression; encoding can be slow; sensitive to the "empty cell problem" in naive implementations; requires careful handling of GPU coordinate systems [S1, S3].
-*   **When to use:** Large time-varying volumetric data, highly sparse volumetric data, or compressing color features in neural radiance fields [S1, S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Not suitable for lossless compression, as the method is inherently lossy [S1]. Encoding speed can be a bottleneck for real-time applications due to iterative codebook optimization [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding is iterative. Each iteration requires $O(N \cdot K \cdot n)$ operations for assignment and $O(N \cdot n)$ for updates, where $N$ is the number of vectors, $K$ is the codebook size, and $n$ is the dimensionality [S1, S3]. The LBG algorithm is known to be slow for high-resolution data [S3]. PCA-based splitting improves speed [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage is $O(K \cdot n + N \cdot r)$, where $r$ is the bit-depth of the index. This is significantly smaller than storing $N \cdot n$ original vectors if $K \ll N$ [S1, S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Can achieve ~100x storage reduction for Volumetric Radiance Fields (NeRF grids) with competitive rendering quality [S2]. For time-varying data, it exploits high spatial and temporal redundancy [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High compression ratio for data with similar sub-blocks; simple decoding (texture lookup); portable to GPU via texture mapping [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression; sensitive to codebook size; original LBG is slow and prone to empty cells; accumulated error in temporal sequences requires I-frames [S1, S3].
+*   **When to Use:** Large volumetric datasets with high redundancy (spatial or temporal); real-time rendering on GPUs where decoding speed is critical [S1, S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Data with low redundancy; applications requiring lossless compression (unless data is highly sparse and identical) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Vector Quantization was developed for volumetric texture compression and hardware accelerated texture decoding in computer graphics applications [S3]. The Linde-Buzo-Gray (LBG) algorithm was introduced in 1980 by Linde, Buzo, and Gray, improving upon scalar quantizers by Max (1960) and Lloyd (1982) [S3]. In volume rendering, it was adapted to handle large volumetric datasets by exploiting spatial and temporal redundancy [S1]. Recent applications include compressing Volumetric Radiance Fields (NeRFs) to reduce storage by approximately 100× [S2].
+Vector Quantization was developed in the context of speech and image compression, with the Linde-Buzo-Gray (LBG) algorithm introduced in 1980, improving upon scalar quantizers by Max (1960) and Lloyd (1982) [S3]. In volume rendering, it was adapted to handle large time-varying volumetric data, leveraging the high spatial and temporal redundancy of such datasets [S1]. Early implementations faced challenges with execution speed and the "empty cell" problem, leading to modifications such as PCA-based splitting [S1, S3]. More recently, VQ has been applied to compressing Volumetric Radiance Fields (NeRFs), achieving significant storage reductions [S2].
 
